          "path": "_m/t/n/c/h/arasbaran-wp/caucasus-part-persian.jpg",
          "filename": "Cover, silk embroidery on cotton. Iran, Caucasus; 1st half of 18th century. Stored at The David Collection, inventarnummer 37-1969.jpg",
          "caption": "Caucasus was part of the Persian Empire until the Treaty of Gulistan was signed in 1813.\n18th-century Caucasian embroideries were frequently dominated by a central polygon, as in this magnificent example. It was accompanied by stylized vegetal ornamentation, trees, and animals, and in fairly rare cases human figures. With their ornamentation and rich but slightly somber palette, they may resemble the Caucasian dragon carpets that emerged from the local nomadic culture.\n\nLike them, these embroideries differ from the more refined and internationally oriented court art that was produced in the main cities of Iran.",
          "alt": "Caucasus was part of the Persian Empire until the Treaty of Gulistan was signed in 1813.\n18th-century Caucasian embroideries were frequently dominated by a central polygon, as in this magnificent example. It was accompanied by stylized vegetal ornamentation, trees, and animals, and in fairly rare cases human figures. With their ornamentation and rich but slightly somber palette, they may resemble the Caucasian dragon carpets that emerged from the local nomadic culture.\n\nLike them, these embroideries differ from the more refined and internationally oriented court art that was produced in the main cities of Iran.",
          "width": 3331,
          "height": 3658,
          "mimeType": "image/jpeg",
          "license": "Public domain",
          "sourceUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cover,_silk_embroidery_on_cotton._Iran,_Caucasus;_1st_half_of_18th_century._Stored_at_The_David_Collection,_inventarnummer_37-1969.jpg",
